.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the single block that mp holds for its
entries, and for the state of each entry.

This is what the pool costs. ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) does not
report that cost, and this is deliberate. A thread-safe pool large enough
to keep a cache for each thread holds a reserve above the count that it
was created with. Its memory therefore exceeds what its usable capacity
accounts for. Use this call to size a pool against a memory budget. You
then do not have to repeat the reserve arithmetic yourself.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the entry block of the pool
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call counts the entry block and nothing else. The handle itself is a
small fixed allocation. The cache of each thread is a separate
allocation. The library makes it when that thread first uses the pool.
The same reserve bounds those caches. An entry that the dynamic fallback
serves is not part of this block at all.
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) accounts for those.

For a pool that ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3) built,
this reports the part of the buffer of the caller that the pool
divided into entries. Such a pool never receives a cache and carries no
reserve. The figure is therefore exactly the element count times the
stride, plus one status byte for each element. The pool allocated none
of it.

The value does not change over the lifetime of the pool.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of the pool. It is therefore a cold-path call.
Do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
